In article <1993Apr14.231816.18525@beaver.cs.washington.edu> aberman@cs.washington.edu (Andrew Berman) writes:
>How about this as a suggestion:
>
>Remove the "eating Wraiths --> increase level".
>
>It shouldn't be easier for me to get from level 14 to level 25 than to get
>from level 1 to level 5.  And this gain level stuff seems like an inherent
>cheat.
>
>If that is too radical, then how about this:
>
>Eating Wraiths --> + or - 1 level, with no luck modification.
>
>And throw in an occasional sickness or poisoning with no immunity
>(like drinking a cursed potion of sickness).

No.  That won't work.

Going up levels starts getting difficult after 9.  Level 14 is very
difficult, and I've never gained level 15 by experience points.  I
usually only find two or three potions of gain level (the monsters
drink the rest).  Wraith corpses are essential.  If they don't raise
levels, there needs to be some other way.

However, there is no reason you should get so many from the Bell of
Opening.  Ringing the Bell shouldn't summon undead.  If it is cursed,
it should simply foul up the (you know what).

However, reading the Book when you're not (you know) does summon
undead.  It is reasonable to eliminate this behavior from the Bell,
but leave it for the Book.  By the time you have the Book, your items
are getting randomly cursed, monsters are suddenly appearing in large
numbers (and attacking), and the Wizard is really, really angry.  My
strategy at this point is to run like Hell (that's where I am, after
all).  If a player wants to take the time with the Book at this point,
it is a tradeoff, and reasonable to allow it.  On the other hand, a
Priest can take the Bell, go up above Gehennom, summon lots of undead,
and just destroy them by turning them.  No Wizard involved....

In short, eliminate the Bell's undead behavior, and just leave it for
the Book.

